EXPERIMENT - 3.1 ->  Implement Queue Using Stacks (232):
AIM: 
Implement a first in first out (FIFO) queue using only two stacks.The implemented queue should support all the functions of a normal queue 
(push, peek, pop, and empty).

ALGORITHM:
1. Start.
2. Create two stacks:
    -> stack1 for storing newly inserted elements.
    -> stack2 for removing elements in queue order.
3. To perform push(x):
    -> Push the element x into stack1.
4. To perform pop():
    -> If stack2 is empty, transfer all elements from stack1 to stack2.
    -> Pop the top element from stack2.
5. To perform peek():
    -> If stack2 is empty, transfer all elements from stack1 to stack2.
    -> Return the top element of stack2.
6. To perform empty():
    -> Return true if both stack1 and stack2 are empty.
    -> Otherwise, return false.
7. The transfer from stack1 to stack2 reverses the order of elements, allowing the queue to follow FIFO order.
8. Stop.

CODE:
import java.util.Stack;

class MyQueue {
    Stack<Integer> in;
    Stack<Integer> out;

    public MyQueue() {
        in = new Stack<>();
        out = new Stack<>();
    }
    public void push(int x) {
        in.push(x);
    }
    public int pop() {
        if (out.isEmpty()) {
            while (!in.isEmpty()) {
                out.push(in.pop());
            }
        }
        return out.pop();
    }
    public int peek() {
        if (out.isEmpty()) {
            while (!in.isEmpty()) {
                out.push(in.pop());
            }
        }
        return out.peek();
    }
    public boolean empty() {
        return in.isEmpty() && out.isEmpty();
    }
}

Learning Outcomes:
1. Understand how to implement a queue using two stacks.
2. Learn the FIFO (First In, First Out) principle of a queue.
3. Perform queue operations (push, pop, peek, and empty) using stacks.
4. Analyze the time complexity of each queue operation.
5. Understand how stack operations can be used to simulate queue behavior.
